This edition of the Scout Master Podcast is very happily sponsored by backers like me. Thanks, Clark.

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
And now it's the old scout master.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Have you heard about the scout who was so interested in knots he would only eat Thai food?

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Oh, you haven't heard about that yet,

6
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Oh dear. Well, this is podcast number 216..

7
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Welcome back to the Scout Master Podcast. This is Clark Green.

8
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Mike Beck wrote in about our post of a week or so ago about scouting as craftsmanship

9
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:22,000
And he said: it's true, we should think of scouting as craftsmanship rather than as a form of institutional or mass production education.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:27,000
We often see instruction as holding a class, but that is not scouting.

11
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Your podcast on Introduction to Leadership Skills for Troops was timely for us.

12
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:39,000
We're revamping the way that we present it as to make it more of a bite-sized and immediately practicable kind of thing.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Our aim is that we won't just present one long training session,

14
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
but we'll have a shorter initial event that will facilitate more active mentoring with the adults and the patrol leaders council.

15
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Thanks for getting in touch, Mike. You know I found something that Biden Powell said after the podcast where we were talking about training youth leaders,

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:09,000
He said this: the method is to lead the boy to tackle the objective of his training and not to bore him with the preliminary steps at the outset,

17
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
which I think addresses the subject very well, don't you?

18
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
I also heard from Rob Knapp, who is with Scout Troop 348 in Reiner, Virginia.

19
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
He says: I've been a scout master for a little over three years now.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Three years ago we thought that a scout run troop meant giving scouts a meeting outline, but it really wasn't their troop,

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,000
They were just following an adult plan.

22
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:44,000
I found your blog about seven months ago and after listening to the podcast for a month, you gave me the courage to work towards becoming a truly scout run troop.

23
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:50,000
I'm happy to report that the patrols are functioning well. Our senior patrol leader is following up with his patrol leaders.

24
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
In our meeting room we have a whiteboard that has upcoming activities listed on it.

25
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
At tonight's meeting one of the parents pointed out a mistake in the schedule

26
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:05,000
and I told the parent. I thought this mistake was a great opportunity and he kind of looked at me strangely.

27
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
I asked the senior patrol leader if he had looked at the whiteboard and he said he hadn't.

28
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:18,000
But just by bringing that to his attention. by the end of the meeting the scribe had corrected the mistake, with no further adult involvement.

29
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:21,000
You know this stuff really works. Thanks again for all of your help.

30
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:27,000
Thank you, Rob, for getting in touch, and Rob had an email question that you're going to hear a little later on too.

31
