and you can get the app on Google Play and at iTunes.

40
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Well, in this edition of the podcast, we're going to talk a little bit about positive peer pressure in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less,

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:34,000
and it's really under seven minutes this week. so I'm reminded of the saying that even a blind pig roots up a truffle every once in a while.

42
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:41,000
And then we've got some email questions to answer and that's going to take up the remainder of the podcast. so let's get started, shall we?

43
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less?

44
00:04:46,000 --> 00:05:02,000
During a visit from a Weebelos den at one of our troop meetings, a scout was available to speak with the parents of the visiting Weebelos,

45
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:09,000
and that week the older scouts had been leading a discussion with our younger scouts about disabilities awareness merit badge.

46
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
One of the parents was impressed that the older boys were leading discussions on the subject and asked this older scout:

47
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:23,000
how do you get the older scouts to do this, How do you get them to lead a discussion with the younger ones on such an important subject?

48
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:30,000
And the scout answered that he had watched the older scouts when he was younger and learned how to do things like lead discussions.

49
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:34,000
It all seemed, you know, pretty natural and unremarkable to him,

50
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:41,000
And I think that this is representative of what I like to call positive peer pressure.

51
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:46,000
It's something that takes a little time to develop if it isn't already present in a troop.

52
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Perhaps the most important influence on building this kind of positive peer pressure is the way that the scout master and the other

53
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:00,000
scouts model the acceptance of and respect of new scouts. when they arrive,

54
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,000
New scouts don't have anything to prove to us, They don't have any tests to pass.

55
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:09,000
They become full members of the troop as soon as they walk through the door.

56
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:17,000
So if we are respectful of each other and we also delegate actual responsibility to our youth leadership

57
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:23,000
and we praise their accomplishments and we accept their failures with grace and equanimity,

58
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
we start sending a message about how people are treated in scouting.

59
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:35,000
Everyone rises or falls to positive or negative expectations of their leaders and their peers.

60
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:44,000
Some of our scouts will be at an age where they don't put a whole lot of importance on the opinion that adults have of them.

61
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
They're in the throes of developing their own personality, going through adolescence,

62
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
and you know what I'm talking about.

63
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:59,000
If you've ever lived in the same house with a 13,, 14-year-old scout, you know exactly what I'm talking about.

64
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:07,000
But when they are in an atmosphere of acceptance and where adults treat them with some respect

65
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:13,000
and adults give them responsibility, they're likely to spend a shorter amount of time

66
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
in that kind of difficult period that all of us go through,

67
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:24,000
If we maintain really positive, high expectations of behavior and performance and spirit,

68
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
that's what we're going to see.

69
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:32,000
If we reinforce those expectations with minimal and tempered and positive, constructive discipline,

70
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:39,000
younger scouts internalize those signals and they see the way older scouts act

71
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,000
and this kind of positive peer pressure is fostered

72
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:47,000
and pretty soon the younger scouts grow older to perpetuate the cycle.

73
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,000
This works both in positive and negative ways.

74
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,000
in the same way that, unfortunately, negative or difficult behavior can spread through a group,

75
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:02,000
Positive and constructive behavior can spread exactly the same way.

76
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:06,000
I encourage you to think in terms of positive peer pressure

77
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:12,000
and talk to your scouts about it and start building that into the culture of your troop

78
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:55,000
E-mail. that is, folks.

79
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:06,000
And here's an answer to one of your e-mails.

80
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:13,000
I got this e-mail question recently and we're going to just keep it anonymous because of the subject matter.

81
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:18,000
If a local business wants to donate money to a local scout troop,

82
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:21,000
what BSA guidelines need to be followed?

83
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:25,000
We would not be a project or event for or at the business,

84
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:29,000
just a simple donation with no strings attached.

85
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:33,000
The troop could spend the money as we see fit: on camping equipment

86
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:37,000
or for paying for camping trips for scouts that need it.

87
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:40,000
We would not promote the business in any way.

88
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
maybe put a thank you on our website or something like that.

89
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:47,000
The answer to that question is a qualified yes.

90
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:51,000
A while back, I had in a blog post a document from the BSA

91
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:57,000
called Fiscal Policies and Procedures for BSA Units.

92
00:09:57,000 --> 00:10:01,000
I will link to it in the post that contains this podcast,

93
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:06,000
but it's one document that every volunteer ought to read at least once

94
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:11,000
and then keep in their resource file so that when questions like this come up,

95
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,000
you'll be able to turn right to it and get your answers.

96
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:19,000
Here's the part of the document that addresses this question specifically.

97
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:24,000
Simply put, units are not permitted to solicit any gifts.

98
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Both the charter and bylaws and the rules and regulations of the BSA make this very clear.

99
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:35,000
Only local councils may solicit individuals, corporations,

100
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:39,000
United Ways or foundations for gifts in support of scouting

101
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:42,000
Units, and unit leaders and youth members

102
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:45,000
may not solicit gifts in the name of scouting

103
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,000
or in support of unit needs and activities,

104
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:54,000
except in unusual circumstances where the unit has received permission

105
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
to do so from the local council.

106
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Units are also prohibited from soliciting gifts on their websites.

107
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:07,000
Now, does that mean that people can't make gifts to your troop.

108
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
I'm still quoting the document here.

109
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:13,000
Units are not supposed to solicit gifts, but they can receive gifts.

110
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:18,000
Anyone can contribute to a scout pack, troop or unit

111
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
and many donors don't need or care about charitable deductions.

112
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,000
Obviously, defining a solicited gift is not always easy,

113
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:30,000
but we rely on our unit leaders to set good examples

114
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
and honor the intent and spirit of these important guidelines.

115
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,000
We know it's hard to stop people from being generous, especially towards scouting.

116
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:43,000
So the qualification in the yes that yes, you can accept a donation to your troop-

117
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:48,000
is if someone came to you of their own volition and offered a donation,

118
00:11:48,000 --> 00:11:50,000
you can accept it.

119
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,000
If they want a tax letter, the letter comes from your chartering organization.

120
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:57,000
If someone in the unit or the chartering organization went out

121
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:01,000
and solicited this donation, well then you've crossed the line.

122
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:06,000
As I just read, only local councils can solicit donations

123
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:10,000
And the idea here, as you can imagine, is if units were permitted

124
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:14,000
to individually go out and solicit and accept donations,

125
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:18,000
you'd end up with some very well-funded units and underfunded councils.

126
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,000
So the idea is we want to not water one specific leaf,

127
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,000
That's the scout troop or the cub pack or the venture crew.

128
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:31,000
We don't want to just benefit them alone.

129
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:35,000
We want the whole tree- and that would be the council- to benefit,

130
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:40,000
And then everybody gets the benefit of those donations rather than just one unit.

131
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:44,000
If your unit was approached with the offer of a donation,

132
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:49,000
I would advise you to keep all of it as transparent as possible.

133
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Get in touch with your local council and explain exactly what's going on

134
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
so there'll be no misunderstandings.

135
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:59,000
Then be sure that everybody involved with the unit understands

136
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:03,000
the rules about soliciting any additional donations,

137
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
because you know people are going to see this donation, come through

138
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
and think it would be a really great idea to go out and solicit others,

139
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
And you have to explain very carefully and definitively that that is simply not allowed.

140
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
In my personal opinion, I think a thank you letter to the donor,

141
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:23,000
hand-delivered by a deputation of uniformed scouts,

142
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:26,000
would be a nice gesture in recognizing the donation.

143
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:29,000
I would advise against mentioning it on a website

144
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,000
or in a newsletter or anything that could be misconstrued

145
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:37,000
as the BSA endorsing a business in any way,

146
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:41,000
As in anything that you hear on the podcast or read on the blog.

147
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:47,000
this is my advice, based on reading the policy statements that I can find

148
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
and my own experience.

149
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:53,000
I strongly urge you to contact your local council

150
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,000
and explain what's happening, to be doubly sure that nothing is misunderstood,

151
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
and to study the policy documents that I'm referring you to

152
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
and make sure that I've got this right.

153
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:06,000
I've been involved with council level fundraising issues

154
00:14:06,000 --> 00:14:11,000
and I've learned that these policies are there for very good reasons.

155
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:17,000
What some misguided volunteers and units do to lavishly fund their leaf,

156
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:21,000
their unit, their small part of the council-

157
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
and I'm talking tens of thousands of dollars-

158
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:26,000
is pretty stunning.

159
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:29,000
It's not that councils are greedy,

160
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:33,000
it's just that when money goes to the council, everyone benefits,

161
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
All the units benefit,

162
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:39,000
And so the rules and policies are there for very good reasons

163
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:44,000
And naturally I recommend that you understand them and follow them.

164
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
Rob Knapp writes in to ask:

165
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:51,000
we'll be having elections for senior patrol leader soon.

166
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
The scouts will be electing the new senior patrol leader

167
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:57,000
and, as you suggest, that scout will appoint his quartermaster,

168
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,000
scribe chaplain's aides, et cetera.

169
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:03,000
Do you suggest that the scouts elect the assistant senior patrol leader,

170
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
or should the senior patrol leader appoint him as well?

171
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Also, should the assistant senior patrol leader be present

172
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
when the scout master and senior patrol leader are meeting together

173
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:15,000
or just act as a fill in when the senior patrol leader isn't around.

174
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Well, your question about how the positions of responsibility are filled

175
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
aren't a matter of opinion or choice.

176
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,000
It's really spelled out very specifically in the scout master's handbook

177
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,000
And quoting from that. the youth leader with the most responsibility

178
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
in a troop is the senior patrol leader.

179
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
He is elected by all members of the troop.

180
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:39,000
So we understand: senior patrol leader gets elected by the other scouts.

181
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
And then the scout master's handbook goes on to say- quote

182
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:45,000
with the approval of the scout master, the assistant senior patrol leader

183
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:48,000
is appointed by the senior patrol leader.

184
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Okay, so we've got that piece.

185
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,000
And then in the scout master's handbook it talks about other troop positions

186
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:00,000
And it says this quote: depending on the size and needs of the troop,

187
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,000
any or all of the following positions may be filled.

188
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:07,000
This is at the head of a listing of descriptions

189
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
of these different positions of responsibility.

190
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
Unless otherwise indicated, the senior patrol leader selects the scout

191
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
who will hold each position.

192
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:19,000
The scout master can help the senior patrol leader

193
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,000
make these decisions on the basis of the candidate's overall qualifications

194
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
rather than merely on popularity or friendships.

195
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:30,000
Scouts serving in any of the following troop positions

196
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:33,000
will also continue to be active members of their patrols.

197
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:36,000
So the handbook then goes on to list the positions

198
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:41,000
of quarter master, scribe, OA representative, historian, librarian, instructor,

199
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:45,000
chaplain, aide, den chief, junior assistant, scout, master, et cetera.

200
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:49,000
And while they use the phrase unless otherwise indicated

201
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:54,000
to modify the idea that the senior patrol leader selects the scout

202
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:57,000
who will hold each position, I haven't found anything

203
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:00,000
in the descriptions of any of those positions

204
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:04,000
that indicate someone else would be appointing those positions

205
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
or those positions would be elected for some reason.

206
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:10,000
So the process you go through with a senior patrol leader

207
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
as he makes those appointments

208
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
is a really great opportunity to work together.

209
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
I usually sit down with a new senior patrol leader

210
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:20,000
and I ask him who he wants to appoint for what position,

211
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:23,000
And in our troop we typically have an assistant senior patrol leader,

212
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
quarter master and scribe

213
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Most of the time. I don't have a whole lot to say about the choices

214
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,000
that he makes, other than you know. that sounds good to me.

215
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
If he's having a tough time choosing,

216
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
I would advise him to start with any scouts

217
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:39,000
who put themselves forward for the senior patrol leader election,

218
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
because the fact that they stepped up

219
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:44,000
is a pretty good indication of their interest and commitment right.

220
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:48,000
So I advise him that he should start with his best choice

221
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
for assistant senior patrol leader

222
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:53,000
and offer him any of the positions that are open,

223
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:56,000
but tell him that he's hoping that he'll take

224
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
the assistant senior patrol leader position-

225
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
and they almost always do-

226
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:03,000
and then the new assistant senior patrol leader

227
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,000
and the senior patrol leader can continue going through

228
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:07,000
and making appointments.

229
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:10,000
As for your second question about whether or not

230
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
the assistant senior patrol leader should be around

231
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
when you're talking to the senior patrol leader,

232
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:17,000
most of the time when I'm talking to my senior patrol leader

233
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
it's in the context of the patrol leader's counsel,

234
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,000
So the assistant senior patrol leader is always there.

235
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
I think the general rule of thumb

236
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
is, when you talk to the senior patrol leader,

237
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
yeah, I think it's a good idea

238
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:31,000
to have the assistant senior patrol leader there

239
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:33,000
because he'll be listening in

240
00:18:33,000 --> 00:18:35,000
and he'll get the same directions, the same mentoring,

241
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:36,000
the same training.

242
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
It's not always practicable,

243
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
but I think it's a great idea when you can make it so.

244
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
Wayne Wilcox is the scout master of Troop 184,

245
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
which is part of the transatlantic council,

246
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:50,000
because Troop 184 is located in Cobham, England,

247
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
So from across the Atlantic,

248
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:55,000
Wayne sent this question.

249
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:56,000
He said

250
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:59,000
I'm proud to say, after awarding three Eagles last year,

251
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
we have another eight scouts who will have earned

252
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:05,000
the rank of Eagle by the end of this program year.

253
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:09,000
We have a policy where we present a scout with his new rank badge

254
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:11,000
immediately after his border review

255
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
at the end of a Troop meeting-

256
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
because our boards of review

257
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
are normally held the same night as Troop meetings-

258
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
And then we recognize them

259
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
at the Troop's regular Court of Honor

260
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:24,000
by presenting the rank certificate and the mother's pin.

261
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
It's been our practice to only allow Eagles

262
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,000
to wear their badge of rank after their Eagle Court of Honor.

263
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:33,000
A couple of this year's Eagles will have about two months

264
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:36,000
between passing their boards of review

265
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
and the next Court of Honor.

266
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:41,000
I'd like these scouts to be allowed to wear their Eagle badge

267
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:44,000
to a council event. that happens after their boards of review,

268
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
but before their courts of honor.

269
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Do you think allowing a new Eagle to wear his patch

270
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:52,000
prior to actually raising his hand for the Eagle charge

271
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and pledge is permissible?

272
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
By the way, love the podcast.

273
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
Well, thanks for getting in touch, Wayne.

274
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:01,000
This is an easy answer because it doesn't involve my opinion

275
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
or my advice.

276
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:07,000
It's all pretty clearly set down in the Guide to Advancement 2013..

277
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
Now, before we talk about that,

278
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:12,000
if you're listening and you don't yet have a copy

279
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
of the Guide to Advancement 2013,.

280
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
get online, search for it-

281
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:19,000
It's available in PDF form- and then read it,

282
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
And that way you'll know the answer

283
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:22,000
to most of these questions,

284
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
or you'll have a pretty good idea

285
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,000
of where to find the answer.

286
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
Back to Wayne's question.

287
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:30,000
we're instructed to recognize scouts as soon as possible,

288
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:34,000
and you're doing that with guys up to Eagle Scout,

289
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:37,000
but we don't create our own rules or traditions

290
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:40,000
that change these instructions.

291
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:43,000
So here we are in the Guide to Advancement.

292
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
here are the big numbers.

293
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Ready,

294
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
You got your pencil,

295
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:49,000
I'll wait.

296
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
Okay, here we go

297
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:56,000
In section 4.2.1.4, titled The Scout is Recognized.

298
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
it reads this:

299
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:01,000
when the Board of Review has approved his advancement,

300
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:05,000
the scout deserves recognition as soon as possible.

301
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
This should be done at a ceremony

302
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
at the next unit meeting.

303
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
His advancement may be recognized again later

304
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
during a formal court of honor.

305
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:16,000
So the only way that Eagle is slightly different

306
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:19,000
from the other ranks is that the application

307
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:22,000
has to be approved at a national level

308
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:26,000
before the insignia is issued to the troop.

309
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
So there's going to be a little more delay there.

310
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:31,000
And when we take a look at Sharpen Your Pencil,

311
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
because here's another section, number

312
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:42,000
8.0.3.0 and titled Particulars for the Eagle Scout rank.

313
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:46,000
the Eagle Scout medal or patch must not be sold

314
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:49,000
or otherwise provided to any unit or to the scout.

315
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:52,000
nor should the court of honor be scheduled

316
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:55,000
until after the certificate is received

317
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
at the Council Service Center

318
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
from the National Advancement Team.

319
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:02,000
So we find that we are instructed

320
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:05,000
to recognize scouts as soon as possible

321
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
after their Board of Review,

322
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:10,000
As soon as the advancement application has been submitted

323
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
and we have the insignia.

324
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
we want to recognize them right away

325
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
And then we can recognize them again

326
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
at the next court of honor

327
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
by presenting them with the certificate,

328
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:23,000
that little card that comes along with the badge.

329
00:22:23,000 --> 00:22:25,000
And you know, if you use the mother's pens,

330
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:27,000
you can do that too.

331
00:22:27,000 --> 00:22:29,000
The only difference with the Eagle is

332
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:31,000
you have to wait a little bit longer

333
00:22:31,000 --> 00:22:33,000
because the National Advancement Team

334
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:35,000
needs to issue the Eagle credentials

335
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:37,000
before any of the insignia can be released to the troop.

336
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:39,000
I also want to note that a court of honor

337
00:22:39,000 --> 00:22:41,000
is nothing more than a formal presentation

338
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:43,000
of the achievement.

339
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:45,000
Nothing official happens

340
00:22:45,000 --> 00:22:47,000
after the Board of Review has concluded

341
00:22:47,000 --> 00:22:49,000
and the certificate for Eagle is issued,

342
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,000
because the Board of Review

343
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:54,000
is the effective date for the rank.

344
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
A scout is an Eagle

345
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
as soon as he walks out of the Board of Review,

346
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
pending the issuance of that certificate.

347
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
The Eagle Pledge

348
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
and similar practices are basically-

349
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
they're just optional traditions.

350
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,000
They're not some kind of official action

351
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:11,000
that makes somebody into an Eagle scout

352
00:23:11,000 --> 00:23:13,000
When there's been a long tradition.

353
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,000
that's kind of contrary to policies like this.

354
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,000
by not allowing scouts to wear

355
00:23:19,000 --> 00:23:22,000
the Eagle insignia until

356
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
they've gone through the Court of Honor

357
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:27,000
and taken one of those traditional

358
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:29,000
Eagle pledges or

359
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:31,000
the Eagle charge,

360
00:23:31,000 --> 00:23:33,000
there's an opportunity to review

361
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:35,000
what we're doing and align ourselves

362
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:38,000
with what we really ought to be doing.

363
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
I've heard the argument about this several times

364
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
that you're not an Eagle and take the pledge

365
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
or receive the Eagle charge

366
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
or some combination of the two,

367
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
but it simply isn't true

368
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
And it never has been to my knowledge.

369
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
I think if this were so,

370
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
the Guide to Advancement 2013

371
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
would explain exactly how that worked,

372
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:59,000
but you'll find that there is nothing there.

373
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,000
If you find any statements contrary to that,

374
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
I'll be glad to tell everyone.

375
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Wayne, I hope that answers your question.

